<h3 id="quasilimit4" class="tex-subsection"><span data-tag="EE5DE9">4.2.4</span> Homotopy Colimits</h3>

<p>
Our goal in this section is to compare the $\infty $-categorical theory of colimits with the more classical theory of homotopy colimits in simplicial categories (see Remark <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>). Our main result is the following: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-theorem" id="EB2834">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/EB2834">Theorem <span data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</span>.</a>colimit!homotopyhomotopy colimit Let $\calC $ and $\calI $ be fibrant simplicial categories and $F: \calI \rightarrow \calC $ a simplicial functor. Suppose given an object $C \in \calC $ and a compatible family of maps $\{  \eta _{I}: F(I) \rightarrow C \} _{I \in \calI }$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The maps $\eta _ I$ exhibit $C$ as a homotopy colimit of the diagram $F$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
Let $f: \Nerve (\calI ) \rightarrow \Nerve (\calC )$ be the simplicial nerve of $F$, and $\overline{f}: \Nerve (\calI )^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \Nerve (\calC )$ the extension of $f$ determined by the maps $\{  \eta _ I \} $. Then $\overline{f}$ is a colimit diagram in $\Nerve (\calC )$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">4.2.4.2</span>.</a>For an analogous result (in a slightly different setting), we refer the reader to <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/hirschowitz">hirschowitz</a>]</span>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
The proof of Theorem <a href="/tag/EB2834" data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</a> will occupy the remainder of this section. We begin with a convenient criterion for detecting colimits in $\infty $-categories: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="CE8F7D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/CE8F7D">Lemma <span data-tag="CE8F7D">4.2.4.3</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category, $K$ a simplicial set, and $\overline{p}: K^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \calC $ a diagram. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
The diagram $\overline{p}$ is a colimit of $p = \overline{p} | K$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(ii)$"><p>
Let $X \in \calC $ denote the image under $\overline{p}$ of the cone point of $K^{\triangleright }$, let $\delta : \calC \rightarrow \Fun (K,\calC )$ denote the diagonal embedding, and let $\alpha : p \rightarrow \delta (X)$ denote the natural transformation determined by $\overline{p}$. Then, for every object $Y \in \calC $, composition with $\alpha $ induces a homotopy equivalence 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \phi _{Y}: \bHom _{\calC }(X, Y) \rightarrow \bHom _{ \Fun (K,\calC ) }( p, \delta (Y) ). \]
</div>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Using Corollary <a href="/tag/B7A1FF" data-tag="B7A1FF">4.2.1.8</a>, we can identify the mapping space $\bHom _{\Fun (K,\calC ) }( p, \delta (Y) )$ with the fiber $\calC ^{p/} \times _{\calC } \{ Y\} $, for each object $Y \in \calC $. Under this identification, the map $\phi _{Y}$ can be identified with the fiber over $Y$ of the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC ^{X/} \stackrel{\phi '}{\rightarrow } \calC ^{ \overline{p}/ } \stackrel{\phi ''}{\rightarrow } \calC ^{p/}, \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $\phi '$ is a section to the trivial fibration $\calC ^{\overline{p}/} \rightarrow \calC ^{X/}$. The map $\phi ''$ is a left fibration (Proposition <a href="/tag/0100E2" data-tag="0100E2">4.2.1.6</a>). Condition $(i)$ is equivalent to the requirement that $\phi ''$ be a trivial Kan fibration, and condition $(ii)$ is equivalent to the requirement that each of the maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \phi ''_{Y}: \calC ^{ \overline{p}/} \times _{\calC } \{ Y \}  \rightarrow \calC ^{p/} \times _{\calC } \{ Y\} . \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a homotopy equivalence of Kan compexes (which, in view of Lemma <a href="/tag/B732E8" data-tag="B732E8">2.1.3.3</a>, is equivalent to the requirement that $\phi ''_{Y}$ be a trivial Kan fibration). The equivalence of these two conditions now follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0628D7" data-tag="0628D7">2.1.3.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
The key to Theorem <a href="/tag/EB2834" data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</a> is the following result, which compares the construction of diagram categories in the $\infty $-categorical and simplicial settings: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="AAC4A7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/AAC4A7">Proposition <span data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</span>.</a>straightening of diagrams Let $S$ be a small simplicial set, $\calC $ a small simplicial category, and $u: \sCoNerve [S] \rightarrow \calC $ an equivalence. Suppose that $\mathbf{A}$ is a combinatorial simplicial model category, and let $\calU $ be a $\calC $-chunk of $\mathbf{A}$ (see Definition <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>). Then the induced map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \sNerve ( \calU ^{\calC } )^{\circ } \rightarrow \Fun (S, \sNerve (\calU ^{\circ }) ) \]
</div>
  <p> is a categorical equivalence of simplicial sets. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">4.2.4.5</span>.</a>In the statement of Proposition <a href="/tag/AAC4A7" data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</a>, it makes no difference whether we regard $\mathbf{A}^{\calC }$ as endowed with the projective or injective model structure. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">4.2.4.6</span>.</a>An analogous result was proved by Hirschowitz and Simpson; see <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/hirschowitz">hirschowitz</a>]</span>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose a regular cardinal $\kappa $ such that $S$ and $\calC $ are $\kappa $-small. Using Lemma <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>, we can write $\calU $ as a $\kappa $-filtered colimit of small $\calC $-chunks $\calU '$ contained in $\calU $. Since the collection of categorical equivalences is stable under filtered colimits, it will suffice to prove the result after replacing $\calU $ by each $\calU '$; in other words, we may suppose that $\calU $ is small. 
    <p>
      According to Theorem <a href="/tag/EDA8B5" data-tag="EDA8B5">2.2.5.1</a>, we may identify the homotopy category of $\sSet $ (with respect to the Joyal model structure) with the homotopy category of $\sCat $. We now observe that, because $\sNerve ( \calU ^{\circ })$ is an $\infty $-category, the simplicial set $\Fun (S,\sNerve ( \calU ^{\circ }))$ can be identified with an exponential $[ \Nerve (\calU ^{\circ }) ]^{ [S] }$ in the homotopy category $\rm {h} \it {\sSet }$. We now conclude by applying Corollary <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
One consequence of Proposition <a href="/tag/AAC4A7" data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</a> is that every homotopy coherent diagram in a suitable model category $\mathbf{A}$ can be “straightened”, as we indicated in Remark <a href="/tag/7402F2" data-tag="7402F2">1.2.6.2</a>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="3E8A7D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/3E8A7D">Corollary <span data-tag="3E8A7D">4.2.4.7</span>.</a> Let $\calI $ be a fibrant simplicial category, $S$ a simplicial set, and $p: \sNerve (\calI ) \rightarrow S$ a map. Then it is possible to find the following: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
A fibrant simplicial category $\calC $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
A simplicial functor $P: \calI \rightarrow \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
A categorical equivalence of simplicial sets $j: S \rightarrow \sNerve (\calC )$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(4)$"><p>
An equivalence between $j \circ p$ and $\sNerve (P)$, as objects of the $\infty $-category $\Fun ( \Nerve (\calI ), \sNerve (\calC ))$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose an equivalence $i: \sCoNerve [S] \rightarrow \calC _0$, where $\calC _0$ is fibrant; let $\mathbf{A}$ denote the model category of simplicial presheaves on $\calC _0$ (endowed with the <em>injective</em> model structure). Composing $i$ with the Yoneda embedding of $\calC _0$, we obtain a fully faithful simplicial functor $\sCoNerve [S] \rightarrow \mathbf{A}^{\circ }$, which we may alternatively view as a morphism $j_0: S \rightarrow \sNerve (\mathbf{A}^{\circ })$. 
    <p>
      We now apply Proposition <a href="/tag/AAC4A7" data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</a> to the case where $u$ is the counit map $\sCoNerve [\sNerve (\calI )] \rightarrow \calI $. We deduce that the natural map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \sNerve (\mathbf{A}^{\calI })^{\circ } \rightarrow \Fun ( \Nerve (\calI ), \sNerve (\mathbf{A}^{\circ } ) ) \]
</div>
    <p>
       is an equivalence. From the essential surjectivity, we deduce that $j_0 \circ p$ is equivalent to $\sNerve (P_0)$, where $P_0: \calI \rightarrow \mathbf{A}^{\circ }$ is a simplicial functor. 
    <p>
      We now take $\calC $ to be the essential image of $\sCoNerve [S]$ in $\mathbf{A}^{\circ }$, and note that $j_0$ and $P_0$ factor uniquely through maps $j: S \rightarrow \sNerve (\calC )$, $P: \calI \rightarrow \calC $ which possess the desired properties. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
We now return to our main result. 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof of Theorem <a href="/tag/EB2834" data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</a>:.</strong>
       Let $\mathbf{A}$ denote the category $\Set _{\Delta }^{\calC }$, endowed with the projective model structure. Let $j: \calC ^{op} \rightarrow \mathbf{A}$ denote the Yoneda embedding, and let $\calU $ denote the full subcategory of $\mathbf{A}$ spanned by those objects which are weakly equivalent to $j(C)$ for some $C \in \calC $, so that $j$ induces an equivalence of simplicial categories $\calC ^{op} \rightarrow \calU ^{\circ }$. Choose a trivial injective cofibration $j \circ F \rightarrow F'$, where $F'$ is a injectively fibrant object of $\mathbf{A}^{\calI ^{op}}$. Let $f': \Nerve (\calI )^{op} \rightarrow \Nerve ( \calU ^{\circ } )$ be the nerve of $F'$, and let $C' = j(C)$, so that the maps $\{  \eta _{I}: F(I) \rightarrow C \} _{I \in \calI }$ induce a natural transformation $\alpha : \delta (C') \rightarrow f'$, where $\delta : \Nerve ( \calU ^{\circ }) \rightarrow \Fun ( \Nerve (\calI )^{op}, \Nerve (\calU ^{\circ }) )$ denotes the diagonal embedding. In view of Lemma <a href="/tag/CE8F7D" data-tag="CE8F7D">4.2.4.3</a>, condition $(1)$ admits the following reformulation: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1')$"><p>
For every object $A \in \calU ^{\circ }$, composition with $\alpha $ induces a homotopy equivalence 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{ \Nerve (\calU ^{\circ }) }( A,C') \rightarrow \bHom _{ \Fun ( \Nerve (\calI )^{op}, \Nerve (\calU ^{\circ })) }( \delta (A),f' ). \]
</div>
</li>
</ul>
    <p>
      Using Proposition <a href="/tag/AAC4A7" data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</a>, we can reformulate this condition again: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1'')$"><p>
For every object $A \in \calU ^{\circ }$, the canonical map 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{ \mathbf{A}}( A,C') \rightarrow \bHom _{ \mathbf{A}^{ \calI ^{op}}}( \delta '(A), F') \]
</div>
<p>
 is a homotopy equivalence, where $\delta ': \mathbf{A}\rightarrow \mathbf{A}^{\calI ^{op}}$ denotes the diagonal embedding. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
    <p>
      Let $B \in \mathbf{A}$ be a limit of the diagram $F'$, so we have a canonical map $\beta : C' \rightarrow B$ between fibrant objects of $\mathbf{A}$. Condition $(2)$ is equivalent to the assertion that $\beta $ is a weak equivalence in $\mathbf{A}$, while condition $(1'')$ is equivalent to the assertion that composition with $\beta $ induces a homotopy equivalence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}(A, C') \rightarrow \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}(A, B) \]
</div>
    <p>
       for each $A \in \calU ^{\circ }$. The implication $(2) \Rightarrow (1'')$ is clear. Conversely, suppose that $(1'')$ is satisfied. For each $X \in \calC $, the object $j(X)$ belongs to $\calU ^{\circ }$, so that $\beta $ induces a homotopy equivalence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  C'(X) \simeq \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}( j(X), C') \rightarrow \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}( j(X), B) \simeq B(X). \]
</div>
    <p>
       It follows that $\beta $ is a weak equivalence in $\mathbf{A}$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="A4ADC1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/A4ADC1">Corollary <span data-tag="A4ADC1">4.2.4.8</span>.</a> Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial simplicial model category. The associated $\infty $-category $S = \sNerve ( \mathbf{A}^{\circ })$ admits (small) limits and colimits. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We give the argument for colimits; the case of limits follows by a dual argument. Let $p: K \rightarrow S$ be a (small) diagram in $S$. By Proposition <a href="/tag/3D6E95" data-tag="3D6E95">4.2.3.14</a>, there exists a (small) category $\calI $ and a cofinal map $q: \Nerve (\calI ) \rightarrow K$. Since $q$ is cofinal, $p$ has a colimit in $S$ if and only if $p \circ q$ has a colimit in $S$; thus we may reduce to the case where $K = \Nerve (\calI )$. 
    <p>
      Using Proposition <a href="/tag/AAC4A7" data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</a>, we may suppose that $p$ is the nerve of a injectively fibrant diagram $p': \calI \rightarrow \mathbf{A}^{\circ }$. Let $\overline{p'}: \calI \star \{ x\}  \rightarrow \mathbf{A}^{\calI }$ be a limit of $p'$, so that $\overline{p}'$ is a homotopy limit diagram in $\mathbf{A}$. Now choose a trivial fibration $\overline{p}'' \rightarrow \overline{p}'$ in $\mathbf{A}^{\calI }$, where $\overline{p}''$ is cofibrant. The simplicial nerve of $\overline{p}''$ determines a colimit diagram $\overline{f}: \Nerve (\calI )^{\triangleright } \rightarrow S$, by Theorem <a href="/tag/EB2834" data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</a>. We now observe that $f = \overline{f} | \Nerve (\calI )$ is equivalent to $p$, so that $p$ also admits a colimit in $S$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>

